---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro'
import JBrowseFigure from '../components/JBrowseFigure.astro'
import { catalog } from '../../../packages/examples/src/examples/catalog'
import { specs } from '../../../scripts/screenshots/specs.mjs'
import { f12CombinedCloseupUrl } from '../lib/f12CombinedLinks'
import {
  brafV600,
  buildOnOpen,
  proteinLinked,
  tp53PhmmerRp15,
  tp53Protein3d,
  tp53R248,
  tp53UnirefBrowser,
} from '../lib/jbrowseLinks'

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL

// The figures and their captions come from the examples catalog, which is also
// what the examples page reads, so a story is written once. Each figure names a
// screenshot spec (scripts/screenshots/specs.mjs), and that spec's `?data=` URL
// is the live link under the image -- the figure and the link therefore open the
// same state by construction. A compose spec stacks other captures and has no
// state of its own, so it gets no link.
const figures = catalog.flatMap(entry =>
  (entry.figures ?? []).map(figure => {
    const spec = specs.find(s => s.name === figure.src)
    if (!spec) {
      throw new Error(`gallery figure ${figure.src} has no screenshot spec`)
    }
    return {
      ...figure,
      example: entry,
      href: spec.url === undefined ? undefined : `${base}/demo/${spec.url}`,
    }
  }),
)
---

<Base
  title="Gallery — JBrowseMSA"
  description="A gallery of react-msaview: alignments that tell a biological story on their own, and connected JBrowse sessions linking genome, alignment, and 3D structure."
  wide
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Gallery</h1>
    <p>
      Two kinds of example: the standalone react-msaview viewer, where the
      biology reads straight off the alignment and tree, and react-msaview
      running inside JBrowse, connected to a genome view and 3D structure. Each
      is built reproducibly from public data by the scripts in the repo.
    </p>

    <h2 id="standalone">Standalone react-msaview</h2>
    <p>
      Just an alignment, a tree, and optional feature overlays — no genome
      browser. Each figure shows a distinct viewer capability: domain overlays,
      reference diffing, data layers, tree topology, RNA secondary structure,
      base-pair arcs, gene arrows, base-resolution DNA, scale. Click one to open
      the demo app in the state it was captured in — the figure and the link
      come from the same screenshot spec. The full set of examples, live and
      interactive with their source, is on the
      <a href={`${base}/examples`}>examples</a> page.
    </p>

    {
      figures.map(f => (
        <Figure src={`${f.src}.png`} alt={f.caption} href={f.href}>
          <strong>{f.title}</strong> — {f.caption}
        </Figure>
      ))
    }

    <h2 id="jbrowse">Inside JBrowse</h2>
    <p>
      react-msaview also runs as a
      <a href="https://github.com/GMOD/jbrowse-plugin-msaview"
        >JBrowse 2 plugin</a
      >: the alignment and tree open <em>inside</em> JBrowse, connected to a genome
      view and — with
      <a href="https://github.com/GMOD/jbrowse-plugin-protein3d">protein3d</a> — the
      AlphaFold structure. Selecting a region in any view lights the matching columns,
      codons, and residues in the others. Each example below is one declarative URL.
    </p>

    <JBrowseFigure
      src="f12-combined-closeup.png"
      href={f12CombinedCloseupUrl}
      alt="A single JBrowse session at base resolution over F12 exon 3: the RefSeq gene annotation track on top and the react-msaview DNA alignment with its species tree below, the four cetaceans showing a gap where every other mammal has a C — the shared single-base frameshift — with the highlighted column linking the genome to the alignment"
      body="Genome ↔ alignment in one JBrowse session: the F12 locus (RefSeq annotation, top) over the react-msaview DNA alignment + species tree, zoomed to base resolution at exon 3. The highlighted column ties the genome to the alignment — the four cetaceans carry a single-base deletion (a gap where every other mammal has a C) that disables the gene. This is the viewer an MSA editor would build on: the 14-exon model is already registered to the alignment columns and lifted onto each species."
    />

    <JBrowseFigure
      src="genome-browser-tp53-protein3d.png"
      href={tp53Protein3d}
      alt="JBrowse: the TP53 gene with ClinVar, the p53 ortholog alignment, and the AlphaFold structure, all connected, with the nuclear export signal motif lit across genome, alignment, and 3D structure"
      body="Genome ↔ alignment ↔ 3D structure: the TP53 gene (RefSeq + ClinVar), the p53 ortholog alignment, and the AlphaFold structure as one connected session, opened with the nuclear export signal motif (residues 339–350) lit across all three — the same motif highlighted on the genome, the alignment column, and the folded structure at once."
    />

    <p>The same connected pattern without the structure view:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href={proteinLinked} target="_blank" rel="noopener">SRC ↗</a> — the Src-family
        kinase alignment beside the SRC gene.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={brafV600} target="_blank" rel="noopener">BRAF V600E ↗</a> — the RAF
        family on the invariant V600 codon, with a ClinVar pathogenic-variant track.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={tp53R248} target="_blank" rel="noopener">TP53 R248 ↗</a> — p53 across
        vertebrates on the R248 hotspot, with ClinVar.
      </li>
    </ul>

    {
      buildOnOpen.live && (
        <>
          <p>
            The links above carry their alignments as files. The two below carry
            a request instead, and the view builds the alignment when it opens:
            the spec names the transcript in <code>connectedTranscript</code>{' '}
            and the plugin looks its exon model up in the genome view's gene
            track, so the whole session is a URL short enough to type.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <a href={tp53UnirefBrowser} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
                TP53 UniRef cluster ↗
              </a>{' '}
              — every reference-proteome protein in UniProtKB within 50%
              identity of p53, looked up from UniProt and aligned to the
              transcript in the browser. No job at any service, so it opens in
              seconds.
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href={tp53PhmmerRp15} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
                TP53 phmmer search ↗
              </a>{' '}
              — the same gene through a phmmer search of UniProt's
              representative proteomes at 15% similarity, the widest net EBI
              offers: p53's relatives across the tree of life. The wait is EBI's
              queue, from seconds to many minutes.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </>
      )
    }

    <h2 id="embedded">Or one panel, in a page you write</h2>
    <p>
      A connected session is the most a reader can be handed and also the most
      they have to take in: three linked views, each with its own tracks, menus
      and coordinate system. When a page has one question to answer, the other
      shape is to embed the viewer alone and put only the controls that question
      needs around it — the alignment without the genome browser, the tree
      turned off when there is no meaningful tree, a diff toggle and an expand
      button beside it.
    </p>
    {
      /* No `#controls-from-props` fragment here: ExampleBrowser reads the hash
      as a route rather than scrolling to an element, so the examples page is
      client:only and carries no such id. hyperlink --check-anchors is right to
      call that a broken anchor, and it fails the build. */
    }
    <p>
      That is a react-msaview component in your own page rather than a JBrowse
      session, and the props drive it live, so a toggle costs no remount and no
      refetch. The <a href={`${base}/embedding`}>embedding guide</a> has the recipe,
      and the <strong>Controls from props</strong> entry on the
      <a href={`${base}/examples`}>examples</a> page is it running.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
